# Files ripgrep skips by default — and therefore what Claude Code's Grep tool
# skips, since it is built on ripgrep. This is NOT .gitignore: everything
# listed here (except the second block, which duplicates real .gitignore
# entries defensively) is committed, tracked, and one `Read` away. It is only
# kept out of *content searches*.
#
# NOTE on this file's provenance: the migration inventory that named this
# file (docs/migration/2026-09-19-clair-conventions-inventory.md) describes
# the donor's `.ignore` as excluding build directories under names like
# `clair_data`/`backend/`/`frontend/`/`node_modules`. The donor's actual
# `.ignore`, read directly, does something different: it excludes frozen,
# superseded-but-still-tracked documentation from search, not build output
# (build output there is already gitignored, and ripgrep already skips
# gitignored paths by default — duplicating them here is unnecessary in the
# common case). This file follows the donor's REAL pattern, applied to this
# repo's own frozen-but-tracked material, and adds the build-artifact
# entries only as defense-in-depth for a context where .gitignore is not
# being honoured (a `--no-ignore-vcs` run, or a plain zip of the tree).

# --- Frozen, superseded prototype material (tracked, not gitignored) ------
# Byte-for-byte copies of the earlier CLI prototype, kept for reference only.
# The live package is worker/src/dna_entropy/; these are never the answer to
# "how does the worker work today," but they use the same vocabulary, so an
# ordinary search pays for both and returns the stale one alongside the
# current one. Recovering something from here for comparison is a `Read`,
# not a search: `rg --no-ignore "<pattern>" worker/legacy/ worker/docs-legacy/`
# if you deliberately want to search the old material anyway.
worker/legacy/
worker/docs-legacy/

# --- Build/dependency output (defense-in-depth; already gitignored) -------
worker/.venv/
app/**/bin/
app/**/obj/
.vs/
Releases/
